  "title": "Rolls-Royce Holdings Plc 2025 Full Year Results",
  "url": "https://www.rolls-royce.com/media/press-releases/2026/26-02-2026-rr-holdings-plc-2025-full-year-results.aspx",
  "snippet": "flow, gives us confidence to announce our first multi-year buyback programme, totalling £7bn-£9bn across 2026 to 2028, of which £2.5bn will be completed in 2026, which includes the £200m that was completed between 2 January and 20 February 2026. [...] Growing shareholder returns: Reflecting strong strategic and financial progress and in line with our capital framework, we reinstated regular shareholder dividends in 2025 and completed a £1.0bn share buyback programme. This represented the first time that Rolls-Royce has paid a dividend in more than five years and the first buyback for 10 years. The final dividend for 2025 is 5.0p per share, taking the total dividend for 2025 to 9.5p, which represents a 32% payout ratio of underlying profit after tax. The final dividend will be paid subject to shareholder approval at our Annual General Meeting on 30 April 2026 1. Our strong balance sheet position, alongside our upgraded mid-term targets for operating profit and free cash flow, gives us confidence to announce our first multi-year"
}
{
  "title": "What's Driving Rolls-Royce's Share Buyback Strategy",
  "url": "https://www.kavout.com/market-lens/what-s-driving-rolls-royce-s-share-buyback-strategy",
  "snippet": "Beyond current performance, future growth drivers are significant. Rolls-Royce's UltraFan technology, targeting the lucrative narrow-body market, could double civil aerospace revenue over the long term, with a demonstrator planned for 2028. The company's selection as the sole provider for the UK's Small Modular Reactor (SMR) program, expected to be profitable and cash flow positive by 2030, opens up a multi-billion-pound revenue opportunity. These initiatives, coupled with strong demand for power generation in data centers (projected 20% annual growth), paint a picture of sustained growth and expanding earnings potential. The buyback itself is seen as a vote of confidence, signaling management's belief in the company's intrinsic value and its commitment to enhancing shareholder returns. [...] 1. Valuation and Growth Expectations:\nRolls-Royce's shares have delivered an astonishing 14-fold return in three years, and the stock currently trades at over 40 times expected 2026 earnings, far above its historical average. This premium valuation implies that investors are already pricing in substantial future growth. The buyback program, while supportive of EPS, needs to be accompanied by continued operational excellence and successful execution of growth initiatives like the UltraFan and SMRs to justify this multiple. Investors should assess whether the anticipated growth from these ventures is sufficient to drive further share price appreciation from current levels. [...] Strategically, the buyback programs communicate management's conviction in the company's long-term earnings power and sustainable free cash flow generation. By reducing the number of outstanding shares, Rolls-Royce aims to boost earnings per share (EPS) and return on equity (ROE), making the stock more attractive to investors. This is particularly important given the company's elevated valuation, as it provides a tangible mechanism to justify premium multiples by enhancing per-share metrics. It also suggests that management views the current share price as an opportune entry point for repurchases, implying a belief that the stock is undervalued relative to its intrinsic worth."
}